# The xLaDe Problem

I get asked regularly — by people seeing xLaDe, AI models analysing repo, and in research paper — why xLaDe is not doing something extraordinary/groundbreaking or too many things. 
Everyone finds xLaDe boring and ordinary. 
So, I thought to write my response collectively and present my thoughts on xLaDe. 
I have presented my reasons but this depends on the reader what he/she may conclude from, not me.

First and foremost, it is due to the history of xLaDe. 
Initially, it wasn’t clear to us (me and my team) what actually would be the contribution. 
In the beginning, the original idea was simple — `we will work on proof assistants`. 
I came up with xLaDe name — x for maths unknown, La for Lakshit, and De for Deepak. 
I had named it xLaDe-Prover. (I was confused whether to name it xLaDe or xLaDe-Prover). 
Naming was easy, but working was impossible. 
We had thought to create our own theorem prover, but we failed horribly and terribly as we were clueless what to actually do. 
We accepted defeat and thought, “we shouldn’t reinvent the wheel. 
Why bother when we can improve and modify other one which is already built.” 
This led us to both Lean 4 and Coq. 
During september 2025, we spent a month discussing entirely whether to pursue Lean or Coq, and reading about their features, syntax, working, environment, etc. 
That time, I thought ocaml spells cool. 
So, the actual initial decision was to work on Coq proof assistant. 
At this time, this work was entirely theoretical and in the form of verbal and physical meetings and discussions.

We eventually decided to work on Coq. 
But the problem was — what to actually do? 
As Coq is written in ocaml and neither I nor my team members were familiar about ocaml, so it made very difficult for us. 
So, we thought to pursue Lean 4 to get out of this problem. 
As a result, Lean 4 arrived in the scene. 
It was more of a replacement of Coq than the preferred choice.

So, the proof assistant was chosen. 
However, what we need to do/what we want to do — this was still unanswered. 
We brainstormed with some ideas — improve Lean 4 language, provide translation of Lean syntax to human language and vice versa, create a Lean 4 IDE, integrate Lean with other things, create a centralized community for Lean inside our project. 
These were all long term tasks and we were not experts or advanced in programming to do this, so what I did, I created project in GitHub, and wrote a few files, like logs, team, etc. (Viewable on branch `initial` in GitHub for historical reasons) and then got busy in semester.

After exams and end of semester, I came back to xLaDe. 
So, I am in my home, thinking what to actually do. 
There are tons of research papers and projects I am reading — all are doing something brilliantly and I felt I wouldn’t be able to work in those projects. 
So, I just make a structure/file tree for xLaDe, and I am like — what if we work on infrastructure of Lean 4? 
I later get to know about the problem Lean 4 is facing like versions drift and compatibility issues, etc. which later become our core target in the xLaDe. 
I wouldn’t explain the xLaDe development here for bloat, but eventually this direction continued. 
So, this is the main reason why xLaDe has such shape and structure.

Second, the xLaDe team is not a professional team. 
I know python and bash. Our CEO, Manpreet knows Python and C, Deepak knows Python too. 
So, we have used Python as our staple language. 
I thought initially to learn Lean 4 language but dropped later due to difficulties and lack of time due to the university schedule. 
Then, it is our first major project. 
I can write some minor programs, solve some problems and that’s all. 
However, a serious project is something I was not confident of building at that time. 
So, a software of xLaDe calibre is not something even I expected to succeed initially and also, it is actually harmful if I try to overcomplicate the project. 
The design of xLaDe is intentionally minimal. 
The documentation and structure may feel bigger more complex or heavy than the implementation and code, but this is something which I would rather be proud of, than a problem. 
This maps directly to our coding and analytical skills.

The structure of xLaDe is modular and I have deliberately designed xLaDe keeping in mind that we will be adding more things in future, and I would be working on xLaDe for years. 
This changes the equation a lot. If we add so many things in xLaDe at the same time, it becomes more difficult to maintain. 
Right now, I know xLaDe by heart, and continue working on it for almost an year (It’s September 1 today), and last 8 months, I have shipped 8 versions, at 1 version a month. 
Even in the research paper, I have mentioned that we will be targeting a release each month. 
This makes maintenance one of the most important task of xLaDe. 
I primarily work on GitHub, then I also have to maintain mirrors — GitLab, Codeberg, Bitbucket, Gitea, Sourceforge. 
After mirrors, I maintain latest torrent file (only I seed in my team currently), then I have our official website of xLaDe to maintain and update. 
In addition, I have posted last 3 versions in Pypi, so it is a substantive work for me. 
I usually work alone, but fortunately most of the work is automated. 
However, I have to keep a look and whenever something breaks, it is me who is accountable for everything and I have to fix everything. 
For example, currently, I am unable to automate pypi release from last two months, and trying to fix it. 
However, the workflow file trigger is getting fixed, so next release I will have to have a look. 
And updating files and links in onion site takes some time and updating website in Tor is easy but still it takes some effort to write and deploy.

But still, I am happy to work on xLaDe and somehow I am able to work with all these constraints and fortunately everything is in control. 
But, if this was too complex project, then it would be difficult to maintain than it is today. 
So, my plan is to find a couple of maintainers if I am able to find, who can help me do regular chores atleast. 
For example — updating version and post release work is tedious but easy, so if someone wants to help, then he/she will be able to do without much problems.

Third, xLaDe is written to support Lean 4 and normally Lean 4 is something which develops rapidly. 
Now, suppose if a user is trying to work on something, he is already confused with toolchain versions, then it wouldn’t be good to confuse him more with xLaDe. 
I want xLaDe to be “BORING AND STABLE”. 
We will make an ecosystem layer around Lean 4 which is invisible. 
Users would not need to bother about xLaDe, they just think xLaDe as a tool to work with in Lean 4. 
xLaDe will help users, not challenge them for a duel. 
As much as we would like to add new and advanced features, we would also like to make xLaDe invisible. 
A good ecosystem tool doesn’t need to add friction, it needs to reduce friction. 
I can’t guarantee or promise xLaDe will be best, but it will be simple. 
We will continue working on making xLaDe better for everyone, and continue listening to feedback, which some projects and corps pretend to, but don’t actually implement [cough, cough].

